1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,250
The bullet hits the pavement six
inches from the lawyer's shoes.

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:04,740
October 1964.

3
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:07,030
Park Avenue, New York.

4
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:08,270
Almost midnight.

5
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:09,030
Rain.

6
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:12,530
Seconds earlier, two men had stepped
out of the dark and grabbed a

7
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:14,250
silver-haired man by the arm.

8
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:15,130
"Come on, Joe.

9
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:18,950
My boss wants to see you." Now
the lawyer is diving for cover.

10
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,200
A car door slams.

11
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:22,390
Tires hiss on the wet street.

12
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:26,990
And Joe Bonanno, one of the most powerful
Mafia bosses in America, is gone.

13
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:31,230
In a few hours, he was supposed to
testify before a federal grand jury.

14
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:33,650
Instead, he vanishes for nineteen months.

15
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:35,750
The FBI can't find him.

16
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:38,510
His own soldiers go to war
in the streets of Brooklyn,

17
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:40,850
not knowing if their
leader is alive or dead.

18
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:44,270
And the men who rule the Mafia have
every reason to want him in the ground.

19
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:47,430
Because one year earlier,
he had tried to kill them.

20
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:49,150
Then, one spring morning,

21
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:51,450
he walks into a federal
courthouse in Manhattan,

22
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:54,070
with a story almost nobody believes.

23
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:57,170
But the strangest part of this
case is not the kidnapping.

24
00:00:57,220 --> 00:01:01,050
Years later, when bombs start exploding
outside Mafia homes in the Arizona

25
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:04,350
desert, the man behind them
will not be a gangster.

26
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:06,190
He will be an FBI agent.

27
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:07,610
This is Black Dossier.

28
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:15,160
And this is the file of
the boss who vanished.

29
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:14,820
Chapter One. The Youngest Boss In America.

30
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:18,800
To understand why anyone would
want Joe Bonanno to disappear,

31
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:20,560
you have to go back thirty-three years.

32
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:23,780
To another night, on the very same avenue.

33
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:25,860
September 1931.

34
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:27,140
High above the avenue,

35
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:29,440
in an office of the New
York Central Building,

36
00:01:29,490 --> 00:01:34,080
a group of men flash badges and walk
in, pretending to be federal agents.

37
00:01:34,130 --> 00:01:35,220
They are killers.

38
00:01:35,270 --> 00:01:37,740
Within minutes, Salvatore Maranzano,

39
00:01:37,790 --> 00:01:41,380
the self-declared boss of all
bosses, lies dead on the floor.

40
00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:43,180
Stabbed, then shot.

41
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:46,620
The order came from a rising
gangster named Lucky Luciano.

42
00:01:46,670 --> 00:01:49,660
That day, Maranzano's
crew lost its leader.

43
00:01:49,710 --> 00:01:53,480
And the man they chose to replace
him was only twenty-six years old.

44
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:54,480
Joe Bonanno.

45
00:01:54,530 --> 00:01:57,980
An orphan from Sicily who had crossed
the sea hidden on a fishing boat,

46
00:01:58,030 --> 00:01:59,460
with no visa.

47
00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:03,580
A bootlegger who had fought at
Maranzano's side in a vicious gang war for

48
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:04,880
control of New York.

49
00:02:04,930 --> 00:02:05,860
Now he had a crown.

50
00:02:05,910 --> 00:02:08,180
And a seat at a brand-new table.

51
00:02:08,230 --> 00:02:10,280
Luciano had created the Commission:

52
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:14,160
a council where America's top crime
lords would settle disputes with votes,

53
00:02:14,210 --> 00:02:16,260
instead of machine guns.

54
00:02:16,310 --> 00:02:18,080
Bonanno was its youngest member.

55
00:02:18,130 --> 00:02:20,480
He kept that seat for
more than thirty years.

56
00:02:20,530 --> 00:02:21,400
No prison.

57
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:22,360
No bullets.

58
00:02:22,410 --> 00:02:24,200
The press called him Joe Bananas.

59
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:25,420
He hated the name.

60
00:02:25,470 --> 00:02:28,200
His empire stretched from
Brooklyn to Montreal,

61
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:30,640
all the way to a house
in the Arizona desert.

62
00:02:30,690 --> 00:02:35,900
But by 1963, almost every man from that
first table was dead or behind bars.

63
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:38,180
The younger men wanted his seat.

64
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:41,050
And he was about to make the
most dangerous bet of his life.

65
00:02:41,898 --> 00:02:44,028
Chapter Two. The Contract.

66
00:02:44,078 --> 00:02:47,768
Around the Commission
table, Bonanno had enemies.

67
00:02:47,818 --> 00:02:48,968
Carlo Gambino.

68
00:02:49,018 --> 00:02:50,248
Tommy Lucchese.

69
00:02:50,298 --> 00:02:54,988
And in Buffalo, Stefano Magaddino,
a ruler who was also his own cousin.

70
00:02:55,038 --> 00:02:59,608
They watched him expand into Canada,
into Arizona, into California.

71
00:02:59,658 --> 00:03:02,768
To them, it looked like
a man building an empire.

72
00:03:02,818 --> 00:03:04,168
Their empire.

73
00:03:04,218 --> 00:03:07,028
What happened next is still
told in two different ways.

74
00:03:07,078 --> 00:03:08,328
But according to the Commission,

75
00:03:08,378 --> 00:03:12,468
and to most historians, Bonanno
decided to strike first.

76
00:03:12,518 --> 00:03:14,948
The plan was simple, and insane.

77
00:03:14,998 --> 00:03:16,068
Kill Gambino.

78
00:03:16,118 --> 00:03:17,068
Kill Lucchese.

79
00:03:17,118 --> 00:03:21,208
Kill Magaddino, and Frank
DeSimone, who ran Los Angeles.

80
00:03:21,258 --> 00:03:25,128
Four murders, and Joe Bonanno would be
the undisputed master of the American

81
00:03:25,178 --> 00:03:26,028
Mafia.

82
00:03:26,078 --> 00:03:27,248
He needed a partner.

83
00:03:27,298 --> 00:03:29,428
He found one in Joseph Magliocco,

84
00:03:29,478 --> 00:03:32,648
the sick, overweight head of
another New York crime family,

85
00:03:32,698 --> 00:03:35,508
a man with his own grudges
against the Commission.

86
00:03:35,558 --> 00:03:38,968
Magliocco gave the job to one
of his most trusted captains,

87
00:03:39,018 --> 00:03:41,588
an ambitious young
mobster named Joe Colombo.

88
00:03:41,638 --> 00:03:43,408
And Colombo did the math.

89
00:03:43,458 --> 00:03:46,268
Killing four bosses was
a gamble with his life.

90
00:03:46,318 --> 00:03:48,808
Betraying the plot was
a shortcut to the top.

91
00:03:48,858 --> 00:03:52,028
He went straight to the targets,
and told them everything.

92
00:03:52,078 --> 00:03:55,288
The Commission summoned both
men to explain themselves.

93
00:03:55,338 --> 00:03:56,428
Magliocco came.

94
00:03:56,478 --> 00:03:57,608
He was terrified.

95
00:03:57,658 --> 00:03:58,728
He confessed.

96
00:03:58,778 --> 00:04:02,008
The bosses decided that he was
not the brains behind the plot.

97
00:04:02,058 --> 00:04:04,928
They let him live, fined
him fifty thousand dollars,

98
00:04:04,978 --> 00:04:06,828
and forced him to step down.

99
00:04:06,878 --> 00:04:10,668
His crew was handed to the man
who had betrayed him, Joe Colombo.

100
00:04:10,718 --> 00:04:13,168
Months later, Magliocco's heart gave out.

101
00:04:13,218 --> 00:04:16,188
But the man they really
wanted to see never showed up.

102
00:04:16,238 --> 00:04:18,228
Joe Bonanno simply did not come.

103
00:04:18,278 --> 00:04:21,738
And in the Mafia, that silence
was louder than any confession.

104
00:04:23,882 --> 00:04:25,540
Chapter Three. Nowhere To Run.

105
00:04:25,590 --> 00:04:28,420
Ignoring the Commission
was a declaration of war.

106
00:04:28,470 --> 00:04:30,680
Bonanno answered it by
leaving the country.

107
00:04:30,730 --> 00:04:35,420
In 1964, he went to Montreal and
tried to become a Canadian citizen.

108
00:04:35,470 --> 00:04:36,680
It did not work.

109
00:04:36,730 --> 00:04:39,840
Canadian police arrested him and locked
him in Bordeaux Prison for almost

110
00:04:39,890 --> 00:04:41,220
three months.

111
00:04:41,270 --> 00:04:42,760
Then they threw him out.

112
00:04:42,810 --> 00:04:45,660
He came back to New York,
and to a new reality.

113
00:04:45,710 --> 00:04:47,420
The Commission had removed him.

114
00:04:47,470 --> 00:04:51,100
Officially, Joe Bonanno was no
longer in command of his own men.

115
00:04:51,150 --> 00:04:54,280
In his place, the bosses
recognized one of his captains,

116
00:04:54,330 --> 00:04:57,320
a quiet, older man
named Gaspar DiGregorio.

117
00:04:57,370 --> 00:04:59,290
The detail that made it hurt:

118
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:02,220
DiGregorio had been best
man at Bonanno's wedding.

119
00:05:02,270 --> 00:05:04,440
And the Mafia was not his only problem.

120
00:05:04,490 --> 00:05:07,200
A federal grand jury in
Manhattan wanted him under oath,

121
00:05:07,250 --> 00:05:10,100
to answer questions about organized crime.

122
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:15,000
So by October 1964, Bonanno
was trapped between two courts.

123
00:05:15,050 --> 00:05:16,560
One could send him to prison.

124
00:05:16,610 --> 00:05:18,120
The other could send him to the cemetery.

125
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:20,460
On the evening of October twentieth,

126
00:05:20,510 --> 00:05:23,860
he sat down to dinner with his
lawyers to prepare his testimony.

127
00:05:23,910 --> 00:05:25,190
He had one night left.

128
00:05:27,116 --> 00:05:29,051
Chapter Four. The Night On Park Avenue.

129
00:05:29,101 --> 00:05:30,951
The dinner ran late.

130
00:05:31,001 --> 00:05:32,991
When the group finally stepped outside,

131
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:37,191
it was close to midnight, and a cold
drizzle was falling on Manhattan.

132
00:05:37,241 --> 00:05:39,971
Bonanno shared a taxi
with two of his lawyers.

133
00:05:40,021 --> 00:05:42,211
One of them, William Power Maloney,

134
00:05:42,261 --> 00:05:45,291
lived on Park Avenue,
at Thirty-Sixth Street.

135
00:05:45,341 --> 00:05:47,671
Bonanno was going to spend
the night at his building.

136
00:05:47,721 --> 00:05:49,091
The cab stopped.

137
00:05:49,141 --> 00:05:50,391
Bonanno and Maloney got out.

138
00:05:50,441 --> 00:05:53,931
According to Maloney, two men
appeared from the shadows.

139
00:05:53,981 --> 00:05:57,131
One grabbed Bonanno and
said, "Come on, Joe.

140
00:05:57,181 --> 00:06:00,291
My boss wants to see
you." Maloney protested.

141
00:06:00,341 --> 00:06:03,511
One of the strangers fired a shot
into the sidewalk at his feet,

142
00:06:03,561 --> 00:06:05,671
and the lawyer threw
himself to the ground.

143
00:06:05,721 --> 00:06:08,951
When he looked up, Bonanno had
been pushed into a waiting car.

144
00:06:09,001 --> 00:06:10,171
The car was gone.

145
00:06:10,221 --> 00:06:13,891
By morning, the story was on
every front page in New York.

146
00:06:13,941 --> 00:06:17,191
A Mafia don, snatched
off a Manhattan street.

147
00:06:17,241 --> 00:06:18,851
The FBI went looking.

148
00:06:18,901 --> 00:06:19,511
So did the police.

149
00:06:19,561 --> 00:06:21,771
So did his own soldiers.

150
00:06:21,821 --> 00:06:24,471
Years later, Bonanno told his own version.

151
00:06:24,521 --> 00:06:27,631
He said the men forced him to
crouch on the floor of the car.

152
00:06:27,681 --> 00:06:29,811
He felt it turn again and again,

153
00:06:29,861 --> 00:06:33,511
then speed onto a highway and over
the George Washington Bridge into New

154
00:06:33,561 --> 00:06:34,411
Jersey.

155
00:06:34,461 --> 00:06:36,191
He said the men in the car were relatives:

156
00:06:36,241 --> 00:06:40,011
Peter Magaddino, and another
cousin, Nino Magaddino.

157
00:06:40,061 --> 00:06:42,451
He said they drove him to a
farmhouse in the countryside,

158
00:06:42,501 --> 00:06:44,851
and kept him there for weeks.

159
00:06:44,901 --> 00:06:48,591
And when a reporter asked him on national
television who was behind it all,

160
00:06:48,641 --> 00:06:50,371
he gave a three-word answer.

161
00:06:50,421 --> 00:06:53,311
"My dear cousin." Stefano Magaddino.

162
00:06:53,361 --> 00:06:56,951
The ruler of Buffalo, and one of
the names on his own kill list.

163
00:06:57,001 --> 00:06:58,511
It is a perfect story.

164
00:06:58,561 --> 00:07:00,381
There is only one problem with it.

165
00:07:01,127 --> 00:07:03,017
One second before the answer.

166
00:07:03,067 --> 00:07:06,997
If this file has you hooked,
subscribe and turn on notifications.

167
00:07:07,047 --> 00:07:10,117
It's free, and it keeps
these investigations coming.

168
00:07:10,167 --> 00:07:12,207
Now, the problem with Joe Bonanno's story.

169
00:07:12,673 --> 00:07:15,183
Chapter Five. Nineteen Months.

170
00:07:15,233 --> 00:07:19,003
By his own account, his captors
released him after several weeks.

171
00:07:19,053 --> 00:07:21,203
But Joe Bonanno did not go home.

172
00:07:21,253 --> 00:07:22,743
He did not call the police.

173
00:07:22,793 --> 00:07:24,323
He did not show up in court.

174
00:07:24,373 --> 00:07:25,043
He disappeared.

175
00:07:25,093 --> 00:07:26,943
Weeks became months.

176
00:07:26,993 --> 00:07:29,003
Months became more than a year.

177
00:07:29,053 --> 00:07:32,883
Rumors said he was buried upstate,
or lying at the bottom of the ocean.

178
00:07:32,933 --> 00:07:36,463
Back in Brooklyn, his organization
was tearing itself apart.

179
00:07:36,513 --> 00:07:40,443
On one side, Gaspar DiGregorio,
the Commission's choice.

180
00:07:40,493 --> 00:07:43,863
On the other, the soldiers who
stayed loyal to the missing man,

181
00:07:43,913 --> 00:07:45,463
led by his son, Bill.

182
00:07:45,513 --> 00:07:47,823
The newspapers called it the Banana War.

183
00:07:47,873 --> 00:07:52,203
In January 1966, DiGregorio's
side asked for a peace meeting,

184
00:07:52,253 --> 00:07:54,883
at a house on Troutman Street in Brooklyn.

185
00:07:54,933 --> 00:07:55,923
It was a trap.

186
00:07:55,973 --> 00:07:58,533
As Bill Bonanno and his
men walked toward the door,

187
00:07:58,583 --> 00:08:01,683
gunmen opened fire from
the windows and the street.

188
00:08:01,733 --> 00:08:05,123
By some accounts, hundreds of
rounds were fired that night.

189
00:08:05,173 --> 00:08:06,743
Not one man was hit.

190
00:08:06,793 --> 00:08:10,323
Four months later, on
a May morning in 1966,

191
00:08:10,373 --> 00:08:13,803
a well-dressed man walked up to the
federal courthouse at Foley Square in

192
00:08:13,853 --> 00:08:16,263
Manhattan, with a lawyer at his side.

193
00:08:16,313 --> 00:08:17,223
Joe Bonanno.

194
00:08:17,273 --> 00:08:18,063
Alive.

195
00:08:18,113 --> 00:08:19,103
Healthy.

196
00:08:19,153 --> 00:08:21,483
Ready to testify that
he had been kidnapped.

197
00:08:21,533 --> 00:08:23,093
Almost nobody believed him.

198
00:08:23,143 --> 00:08:26,703
Investigators pointed out that the
Magaddinos had been under FBI surveillance

199
00:08:26,753 --> 00:08:28,103
during that period.

200
00:08:28,153 --> 00:08:31,763
Moving a kidnapped don across the state
without the bureau noticing would have

201
00:08:31,813 --> 00:08:33,683
been close to impossible.

202
00:08:33,733 --> 00:08:36,263
Most historians reached
the same conclusion:

203
00:08:36,313 --> 00:08:38,383
the kidnapping was probably staged.

204
00:08:38,433 --> 00:08:42,523
A disappearing act, to escape both
the grand jury and the Commission.

205
00:08:42,573 --> 00:08:45,583
Even his son described those
weeks in strange words.

206
00:08:45,633 --> 00:08:46,683
Not a nightmare.

207
00:08:46,733 --> 00:08:49,363
More like a rest, as his cousin's guest.

208
00:08:49,413 --> 00:08:50,963
Joe Bonanno was back.

209
00:08:51,013 --> 00:08:53,853
But the war he left behind
was not finished with him.

210
00:08:54,607 --> 00:08:57,897
Chapter Six. Bombs In The Desert.

211
00:08:57,947 --> 00:08:59,817
His return did not end the fighting.

212
00:08:59,867 --> 00:09:01,057
It made it worse.

213
00:09:01,107 --> 00:09:05,427
Over the next two years, men from both
sides were shot in Brooklyn and Queens.

214
00:09:05,477 --> 00:09:09,677
DiGregorio was wounded by
machine-gun fire, then fell ill.

215
00:09:09,727 --> 00:09:13,817
The Commission replaced him with
another loyal choice, Paul Sciacca.

216
00:09:13,867 --> 00:09:18,177
Then, in 1968, Bonanno's body
made the decision for him.

217
00:09:18,227 --> 00:09:19,597
He suffered a heart attack.

218
00:09:19,647 --> 00:09:21,257
He sent word to the Commission:

219
00:09:21,307 --> 00:09:25,717
he would retire, leave New York forever,
and live in his house in Tucson.

220
00:09:25,767 --> 00:09:27,237
The bosses accepted.

221
00:09:27,287 --> 00:09:29,397
After four years, the war was over.

222
00:09:29,447 --> 00:09:31,877
The desert was supposed to be his exile.

223
00:09:31,927 --> 00:09:33,197
It became a target.

224
00:09:33,247 --> 00:09:36,417
On the night of July twenty-first, 1968,

225
00:09:36,467 --> 00:09:40,057
an explosion ripped through a
garage on a ranch outside Tucson,

226
00:09:40,107 --> 00:09:43,537
owned by Peter Licavoli, a
leader of the Detroit Mafia.

227
00:09:43,587 --> 00:09:47,357
The next night, two bombs went
off outside Joe Bonanno's home,

228
00:09:47,407 --> 00:09:49,517
knocking down a wall of his patio.

229
00:09:49,567 --> 00:09:54,157
More bombs followed across the city,
all aimed at men connected to the Mafia.

230
00:09:54,207 --> 00:09:55,317
Nobody was killed.

231
00:09:55,367 --> 00:09:57,097
But the message seemed clear.

232
00:09:57,147 --> 00:09:59,017
Someone wanted a new mob war.

233
00:09:59,067 --> 00:10:02,977
When the bombers were caught, the truth
was stranger than anyone imagined.

234
00:10:03,027 --> 00:10:05,757
Testimony pointed to the
man behind the campaign:

235
00:10:05,807 --> 00:10:08,877
David Hale, a special agent of the FBI,

236
00:10:08,927 --> 00:10:12,237
the bureau's own organized
crime specialist in Tucson.

237
00:10:12,287 --> 00:10:13,757
According to the witnesses,

238
00:10:13,807 --> 00:10:16,777
his plan was to make the mobsters
believe they were attacking each other,

239
00:10:16,827 --> 00:10:18,997
and let them finish the job.

240
00:10:19,047 --> 00:10:21,857
Some of those who planted
the bombs were convicted.

241
00:10:21,907 --> 00:10:23,117
Hale left the bureau.

242
00:10:23,167 --> 00:10:24,717
He never faced trial.

243
00:10:24,767 --> 00:10:29,017
Bonanno had survived the Commission,
a mob war, and the federal government.

244
00:10:29,067 --> 00:10:32,017
Now he had survived an
FBI agent's private war.

245
00:10:32,067 --> 00:10:35,227
And then, the old man did
something nobody saw coming.

246
00:10:37,228 --> 00:10:39,395
Chapter Seven. The Book
That Broke The Commission.

247
00:10:39,445 --> 00:10:43,295
In 1983, Joe Bonanno
published his life story.

248
00:10:43,345 --> 00:10:44,875
He called it A Man of Honor.

249
00:10:44,925 --> 00:10:47,615
A former Mafia chief,
writing under his own name,

250
00:10:47,665 --> 00:10:50,755
about the secret world
he swore never to reveal.

251
00:10:50,805 --> 00:10:52,605
He described the Mafia's traditions.

252
00:10:52,655 --> 00:10:54,855
And he described the Commission:

253
00:10:54,905 --> 00:10:59,475
the ruling council of bosses, its
rules, its members, its meetings.

254
00:10:59,525 --> 00:11:01,035
He went on 60 Minutes,

255
00:11:01,085 --> 00:11:02,795
sat across from Mike Wallace,

256
00:11:02,845 --> 00:11:06,735
and talked for the cameras, without
ever admitting to a single crime.

257
00:11:06,785 --> 00:11:10,635
In New York, a federal prosecutor
read the book very carefully.

258
00:11:10,685 --> 00:11:12,495
His name was Rudy Giuliani.

259
00:11:12,545 --> 00:11:16,035
For decades, the government had struggled
to prove that a national ruling body

260
00:11:16,085 --> 00:11:17,995
of the Mafia even existed.

261
00:11:18,045 --> 00:11:20,995
Now one of its founding members
had described it in print.

262
00:11:21,045 --> 00:11:24,855
The book helped inspire one of the
biggest prosecutions in American history:

263
00:11:24,905 --> 00:11:26,355
the Commission case.

264
00:11:26,405 --> 00:11:30,935
In 1986, the leaders of several New
York crime families were convicted,

265
00:11:30,985 --> 00:11:33,355
and sentenced to a
hundred years in prison.

266
00:11:33,405 --> 00:11:36,275
Giuliani wanted Bonanno
on the witness stand.

267
00:11:36,325 --> 00:11:37,875
Bonanno refused.

268
00:11:37,925 --> 00:11:38,795
At eighty years old,

269
00:11:38,845 --> 00:11:40,695
he was jailed for contempt of court,

270
00:11:40,745 --> 00:11:45,095
and spent fourteen months behind
bars rather than break his silence.

271
00:11:45,145 --> 00:11:47,855
He went home to the desert in 1986.

272
00:11:47,905 --> 00:11:53,375
Joseph Bonanno died in Tucson, on
May eleventh, 2002, at ninety-seven.

273
00:11:53,425 --> 00:11:57,855
A man who had once sat beside
Lucky Luciano died in his own bed,

274
00:11:57,905 --> 00:12:00,495
outliving almost every rival
who had ever wanted him dead.

275
00:12:00,545 --> 00:12:02,555
He never changed his story.

276
00:12:02,605 --> 00:12:07,775
Until the end, he swore that on that
rainy night in 1964, he was kidnapped.

277
00:12:07,825 --> 00:12:08,895
So which is it?

278
00:12:08,945 --> 00:12:11,455
Was the boss who vanished
really a prisoner?

279
00:12:11,505 --> 00:12:16,275
Or did he stage the most famous disappearance
in Mafia history, and walk away?

280
00:12:16,325 --> 00:12:19,415
Tell me in the comments:
kidnapped, or staged?

281
00:12:19,465 --> 00:12:22,055
And if this file kept you
watching until the end,

282
00:12:22,105 --> 00:12:24,255
subscribe and turn on notifications.

283
00:12:24,305 --> 00:12:26,875
A new Black Dossier file is coming soon.

284
00:12:26,925 --> 00:12:27,985
This one is closed.
